<p>If you are 22 and your beard comes in thick on the chin and moustache but barely at all on the cheeks, the most likely explanation is that it has not finished developing. Facial hair is one of the later changes of adolescence, and in many men density keeps increasing into the mid-twenties, sometimes later. So for most people at that age the honest first answer is to wait, and to wait in a way that produces evidence rather than frustration.</p>

<p>Waiting is not the whole answer, though. Some patchy beards are not developmental at all: alopecia areata can cause smooth patches in the beard and has to be ruled out, and scars, infection and hair-pulling can all leave gaps. Treatment has a limited but real place. And a transplant is a reasonable choice for some men, usually later, once the pattern has stopped changing and the donor arithmetic has been done. This article sets out how to tell which situation you are in.</p>

<h2 id="why-a-beard-at-22-is-often-unfinished">Why a beard at 22 is often unfinished</h2>

<p><strong>Beard growth depends on how facial follicles respond to androgens, and that response matures slowly.</strong> Many men see the moustache and chin fill in first, with the cheeks and the strip connecting moustache to beard following over several years. A beard that looks patchy at 20 or 22 may look quite different at 26.</p>

<p>How full a beard eventually becomes is mainly genetic. Men with a sparse beard usually have normal testosterone levels; what differs is how responsive the follicles in each part of the face are to the hormones already present. That is why the beards of your father, uncles and older brothers tell you more than any supplement label does.</p>

<p>It also explains the usual shape of patchiness. The upper cheeks and the gap between moustache and chin are commonly the last zones to fill, and in some men they never do. Neither outcome is a medical problem. The question is only which of the two you are looking at, and at 22 it is usually too early to know.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-tell-whether-your-gaps-are-stable">How to tell whether your gaps are stable</h2>

<p><strong>The only reliable way to know whether a patchy beard is still developing is to compare it with itself over time.</strong> Photographs taken every three months, in the same light, from the same angles and at the same beard length, will show within a year or so whether the gaps are narrowing. Memory and a bathroom mirror will not.</p>

<h3>Track it properly</h3>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Same light.</strong> Face a window in daylight, or stand under the same lamp every time. Overhead light exaggerates gaps; light from the side hides them.</li>
  <li><strong>Same angles.</strong> Front, both sides, and chin raised, with the phone held at the same distance.</li>
  <li><strong>Same length.</strong> Stubble exposes patchiness and a longer beard covers it, so trim to the same guard length a few days before each set, or only compare photographs taken at one length.</li>
  <li><strong>Every three months.</strong> Beard density changes slowly, and monthly photographs mostly record noise.</li>
</ol>

<p>The same discipline is what makes any hair comparison honest, which is covered in <a href="/blog/how-to-read-before-and-after-photos">how lighting and angle distort before-and-after photos</a>.</p>

<h3>Signs a beard is probably still developing</h3>

<ul>
  <li>You are in your late teens or early twenties.</li>
  <li>Fine, light hairs are visible in the gaps under good light, and some are getting darker or coarser.</li>
  <li>The edges of the patches are moving inward across your photographs.</li>
  <li>Older men in your family ended up with fuller beards than you have now.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Signs the pattern has probably settled</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Your photographs show no meaningful change across a year or more.</li>
  <li>You are in your mid-twenties or older.</li>
  <li>The gaps are genuinely bare skin rather than fine hair.</li>
  <li>The pattern matches men in your family whose beards never filled either.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of these is decisive on its own. Taken together, over time, they are far more useful than a guess made from a single photograph.</p>

<h2 id="rule-out-what-is-not-genetics">Rule out what is not genetics first</h2>

<p>A beard that never filled in and a beard that has lost hair are different problems. If a patch appeared where hair used to grow, or you are simply not sure, it needs examining before anyone talks about waiting, treatment or grafts.</p>

<h3>Alopecia areata in the beard</h3>

<p><strong>Alopecia areata is an autoimmune condition that can cause smooth, well-defined bald patches in the beard, sometimes called alopecia barbae.</strong> It has to be ruled out before a patchy beard is treated as a cosmetic question, because it follows a different course, is treated differently, and grafts placed into an active patch can be lost.</p>

<p>The <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/hair-loss/types/alopecia" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">American Academy of Dermatology</a> describes alopecia barbae as patchy hair loss on the beard. Clues that point towards it rather than an unfinished beard include patches that appeared over weeks where hair previously grew, skin that looks smooth and otherwise normal, a patch that is enlarging or new patches appearing, and hair loss elsewhere, such as the scalp or eyebrows. Under magnification the edges of an active patch often show short, broken hairs of a characteristic shape, which is one reason an examination settles what a photograph cannot.</p>

<p>Alopecia areata can regrow on its own or with treatment, and it can also relapse. It is managed medically, and a transplant is considered, if at all, only after a long period of stability. <a href="/alopecia-areata-treatment-in-gurgaon">How alopecia areata is assessed and treated at the clinic</a> covers the approach, and the same caution applies to <a href="/eyebrow-transplant-in-gurgaon">eyebrow transplants</a>, where the condition also shows up.</p>

<h3>Other causes worth excluding</h3>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Scars</strong> from acne, injury or surgery, which leave hairless skin that no amount of waiting will fill.</li>
  <li><strong>Infection or inflammation of beard follicles,</strong> including fungal infection, which tends to be itchy, red or pustular rather than quiet.</li>
  <li><strong>Hair pulling or picking,</strong> a habit that often happens with little awareness and leaves irregular patches of broken hair.</li>
  <li><strong>Hair removal that went too far,</strong> such as laser treatment or aggressive threading along the cheek line.</li>
</ul>

<p>Finally, a very sparse beard alongside other signs that puberty has not fully progressed is a question for a doctor about hormones and development, not a cosmetic one. That is uncommon, but it should not be missed.</p>

<h2 id="what-treatment-can-and-cannot-do">What treatment can and cannot do</h2>

<p>If the beard is simply sparse and still developing, the options between doing nothing and surgery are limited, and it is worth being clear about which of them have evidence behind them.</p>

<h3>Topical minoxidil</h3>

<p>Minoxidil is a scalp hair-loss medicine that is sometimes used off-label on the beard. The evidence is thin but not absent. A 2026 randomised, placebo-controlled trial in <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41778417/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">69 transgender men on stable testosterone therapy</a> found that twelve weeks of topical 3% minoxidil improved beard and moustache density compared with placebo, with adverse events comparable between the groups. Published trials in other groups of men are few.</p>

<p>That is a useful signal with real limits. The trial was short, it studied a specific group already receiving testosterone, and it does not tell you whether any gain lasts once treatment stops; on the scalp, the benefit of minoxidil is generally lost when it is discontinued. It is a prescription medicine in India, it can irritate facial skin, and it is better started after an examination than from an online order.</p>

<h3>Serums, rollers, oils and supplements</h3>

<p>Beard oils condition hair and skin; they do not create follicles. Derma rollers and beard growth serums have little published evidence for beards specifically. Biotin and multivitamins help only where there is a genuine deficiency, which is uncommon, and biotin can interfere with some blood test results. None of these is dangerous in ordinary use, but none should be the reason you put off finding out what your patches actually are.</p>

<h3>Testosterone and anabolic steroids</h3>

<p>Taking testosterone or anabolic steroids to grow a beard is a poor trade. In men whose hormone levels are normal it is not a recognised treatment for a sparse beard, and it carries real risks, including suppression of the body's own hormone production and of fertility, and faster scalp hair loss in men predisposed to it. If there is a genuine hormonal problem, a doctor diagnoses and manages it.</p>

<h2 id="why-grafting-an-unfinished-beard-can-look-strange">Why grafting an unfinished beard can look strange later</h2>

<p><strong>A beard transplant designed around the gaps you have at 22 is designed around a beard that may not exist at 27.</strong> If native hair later fills in around the grafts, the transplanted zones can end up denser, differently textured or out of shape relative to the beard that grew naturally, and grafts cannot be taken back.</p>

<p>Three things tend to go wrong. The cheek line drawn today may sit too low or too high once your own upper-cheek growth arrives. Transplanted scalp hair, which stays finer and straighter than beard hair, becomes more noticeable when surrounded by coarse native growth that was not there at the time of surgery. And donor hair spent on zones that would have filled anyway cannot be recovered for anything else. <a href="/blog/beard-transplant-what-to-expect">What scalp hair does and does not do on your face</a> explains the texture point in more detail.</p>

<p>None of this makes a transplant in your twenties wrong in principle. It makes an unexamined one premature.</p>

<h2 id="the-donor-question">The donor question matters more at 22 than at 35</h2>

<p><strong>Beard grafts come from the same scalp donor area that would be used to treat future scalp hair loss, and at 22 nobody knows what your scalp will need.</strong> A transplant at that age spends from a finite budget before its largest possible demand has shown itself.</p>

<p>By 35, a man's pattern of scalp loss is often clearer, and so is the family pattern he is likely to follow. At 22 there may be early temple recession or nothing at all, and neither settles the future. That is not a reason to refuse beard work to young men. It is a reason to measure donor supply, discuss family history plainly and keep the beard plan conservative. <a href="/blog/donor-area-limits-hair-transplant">Why the donor area is a one-time budget</a> sets out how supply is estimated, and the possibility of a future <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">scalp hair transplant</a> belongs in the conversation even if you have no plans for one.</p>

